# Mobile structure standards

Related: [mobile-ui-consumption.mdc](mobile-ui-consumption.mdc), [code-cleanliness.mdc](code-cleanliness.mdc).

## Top-level layout

- **`mobile/app/`** — Expo Router only (thin route files that re-export screens). No business logic.
- **`mobile/src/features/`** — domain modules (auth, shell, sms, email, payment, …).
- **`mobile/src/shared/`** — cross-feature APIs, env, types (`apiClient`, `secureStorage`, `*AdminApi.ts`).

## Feature module contract

Each feature under `src/features/<domain>/`:

| Folder | Purpose |
|--------|---------|
| `screens/` | Route-level screens (e.g. `QueueScreen.tsx`) — mobile equivalent of web `pages/` |
| `components/` | Feature-scoped UI (dialogs, form sections) |
| `services/` | API wrappers when not in `shared/` |
| `schemas/` | Zod validation for this feature |
| `hooks/` | Feature hooks |
| `utils/` | Feature helpers |
| `types/` | Feature types |

## Routing convention

```text
mobile/app/(app)/sms/queue.tsx  →  export { QueueScreen } from '@/features/sms/screens/QueueScreen'
```

Keep `app/` files to one or two lines. Screen logic lives in `src/features/`.

## Imports

- Use **`@/`** for modules under `mobile/src/` (e.g. `@/shared/services/apiClient`, `@/features/sms/screens/QueueScreen`).
- Use **`@webonone/mobile-ui`** for UI primitives.
- Relative imports only for siblings within the same feature folder when that matches existing patterns.

## Cross-feature dependencies

- Shared APIs and types → `mobile/src/shared/`.
- Do not import another feature's internals across feature boundaries — expose via `shared/` when needed.

## Verification

```bash
npm run type-check -w @webonone/mobile
```
